\date{2026-09-23}
\title{Unitality of upper 2-Segal types}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{stt-macros}
\meta{source}{\PR{335}}
\tag{changelog}

\subtree{
\title{Summary}

\p{
[[theofanischatzidiamantis]] recently approached me with a question about a proof
sketch I had shared a few years prior, which was supposed to demonstrate a décalage
result for 2-Segal types. It turned out he had found a hole in my argument,
essentially because I had conflated the following two diagram shapes among simplicial
types.
}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
0 && 1 &&2 &&& 0 && 1 && 2 \\
\\
 && 3 && &&& 3 && 3 && 3
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-5]
\arrow[from=1-8, to=3-8]
\arrow[from=1-10, to=3-10]
\arrow[from=1-12, to=3-12]
\arrow[from=1-8, to=1-10]
\arrow[from=1-10, to=1-12]
\arrow[from=3-8, to=3-10, equals]
\arrow[from=3-10, to=3-12, equals]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{
An [\em{upper 2-Segal type}](bak-C5E1) is a type that
is local at the inclusion of the left hand shape into the 3-simplex #{Δ³}. And,
following Dyckerhoff–Kapranov \citet{Theorem 6.3.2}{DK19}, the décalage
characterisation states the following.
}

\subtree[bak-EE4C]{
\title{Décalage characterisation of upper 2-Segal types}
\taxon{Theorem}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-23}

\p{
A ([simplicial](stt-00AC)) type #{A} is upper 2-Segal if and only if
#{\Sigma1{x : A}{\Hom_A(x,a)}} is [Segal](stt-00AI) for all #{a : A}.
}
}

\p{
If we expand the Segal condition on the type #{\Sigma1{x : A}{\Hom_A(x,a)}} we may
observe that we get diagrams of the right hand shape and not the left hand shape,
which was the precise gap in my proof. If we could just somehow contract away the
degenerate triangles into the dashed arrows:

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
0 && 1 && 2 \\
\\
3 && 3 && 3\nsp{,}
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-3, dashed]
\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-3, dashed]
\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-5]
\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3, equals]
\arrow[from=3-3, to=3-5, equals]
\end{tikzcd}
}

that would perfectly close the difference between these two shapes (at least among
[triangulated types](stt-00LD)). Contractibility of such degenerate triangles is
equivalent to asking that composites with an identity on the left exist uniquely,
and very luckily for me, Feller, Garner, Kock, Proulx, and Weber \citek{FGKPW21} have
shown that this is the case! So in this contribution I define upper 2-Segal types,
and formalise that [they are left unital](bak-37B9), following their argument.
}
}

\subtree{
\title{New modules}
\ul{
\li{\ref{Synthetic.Tetrahedra}}
\li{\ref{Synthetic.HigherSegal.Upper2Segal}}
}
}
